+# LOSSY-VERIFICATION PILOT PATCH (not upstream vLLM).
+#
+# (Reducible) Fuzzy Speculative Decoding (r-fuzzy), Holsman et al. 2025,
+# "Fuzzy speculative decoding for a tunable accuracy-runtime tradeoff", as
+# unified into Xia et al. 2026's taxonomy (arXiv:2607.08690 Eq. 10, Table 2).
+# Per position, either trust the drafter completely or run the strict test:
+#
+#   pi_rej(v) = pi_res(v) = q(v)  if Div(p, q) < alpha
+#                          = p(v)  otherwise
+#
+# Div is Jensen-Shannon divergence, the paper's own recommendation (avoids
+# the divide-by-zero KL-divergence can hit under non-overlapping top-p/top-k
+# support). alpha -> -inf makes "Div < alpha" always false, recovering strict
+# spec-dec exactly (same convention as this repo's spec-casc-opt patch);
+# alpha -> +inf always trusts q.
+#
+# Reducible, not the paper's own "fuzzy": the paper's own presentation picks
+# q or p by the divergence test and samples directly from whichever is
+# picked. That is equivalent in distribution to running the *ordinary*
+# accept/residual machinery with the same target (q or p) substituted in --
+# Xia et al. prove this in their Appendix C (Result 1) and additionally show
+# the reducible form has provably-greater-or-equal per-token acceptance for
+# the SAME output distribution, since when the target is q, this substitution
+# accepts the drafted token outright (pi_rej(x)/q(x) == 1) instead of
+# resampling from q "from scratch". This patch implements only the reducible
+# form, per that result.
+#
+# Structurally this mirrors the spec-casc-opt patch in this repo almost
+# exactly: both binary-switch the whole per-token target between q and p
+# based on a full-vocab reduction computed once in plain PyTorch before
+# kernel launch (a JS divergence here, TV/argmax there), passed in as a
+# boolean per-token mask. Neither ever blends q and p, and picking q always
+# means "accept unconditionally" (pi_rej(x)/q(x) == 1), so -- like
+# spec-casc-opt -- this patch does NOT need to touch recovery/residual
+# sampling: rejection can only happen on the strict-test branch, where the
+# residual target is p, already vLLM's stock behaviour.
